    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a78826752f2e2840152f6563b9f01df&id=8a7887aca18473d901a1c1350b83549a": {
      "title": "Advisor & Special Programs Manager",
      "description": "Dominican University Careers Careers JOIN OUR TEAM Position: Advisor & Special Programs Manager Location: River Forest, IL Job Id: 2423 # of Openings: 1 JOB TITLE: Advisor & Special Programs Manager DEPARTMENT: Office of the Provost REPORTS TO: Associate Provost for Adult Education and Special Programs POSITION SUMMARY The Advisor & Special Programs Manager is responsible for providing operational coordination of a diverse portfolio of non-traditional programs, including but not limited to, the Online Associate of Arts (AA) program, the Bachelor of Professional Studies (BPS) program, and customized credit and noncredit training programs.  In addition, this position will also lead academic advising and program transition efforts for adult learners, non-traditional students, and/or special populations. This role blends high-level program coordination with student advising responsibilities, ensuring seamless communication, efficient operations, and a high-quality student and partner experience. The position is designed to ensure student persistence, successful program navigation, and efficient progression from entry through completion, with a strong emphasis on removing barriers for non-traditional learners and supporting college and work readiness for special populations. EXPECTATIONS FOR ALL DOMINICAN EMPLOYEES: To support the University's mission of preparing students to pursue truth, to give compassionate service, and to participate in the creation of a more just and humane world. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Program Coordination Provide coordination and administrative support for university special programs Coordinate Dominican University’s Alpha Sigma Lambda chapter, overseeing chapter operations, event planning, induction ceremonies, and student engagement initiatives Support delivery logistics for off-site or partner-based programming Support pathway alignment between Adult AA, BPS, and other academic programs Collaborate with program coordinators, registrar, recruitment, admissions, and partner high schools to support student success Collaborate regularly with supervisor and appropriate university staff to review and build semester schedules Contribute to the development of advising materials, pathway maps, and student success resources Support continuous improvement initiatives related to student retention, completion, and transition outcomes Build and maintain relationships with key external partners, including local community colleges and organizations, employers, and workforce agencies Occasionally represent the university at events, college fairs, community events, employer sites, and partner locations geared towards adult and non-traditional learners Advising & Transitions Provide holistic academic advising to adult learners and special populations enrolled in non-traditional credit and noncredit programs, including degree planning, course selection, and progression mapping Provide front-line support and serve as a consistent point of contact for assigned student populations Conduct degree audits and monitor student progress toward completion Assist students in understanding program requirements, institutional policies, and academic expectations Conduct regular check-ins/advising meetings to support academic success and persistence Identify and address barriers impacting student success, particularly for adult, first-generation, and non-traditional learners and guide them through academic challenges, connecting them with appropriate institutional resources (e.g., tutoring, financial aid, student services) Promote retention and completion through proactive outreach and intervention strategies Support students in understanding college expectations, course rigor, and degree pathways Maintain a routine presence at partner off-site locations to ensure quality student services and alignment with institutional standards through student engagement and support Maintain accurate advising records and student data in institutional systems Ensure a high level of customer service and responsiveness across all program areas Additional Responsibilities Other duties as assigned by supervisor QUALIFICATIONS Required Bachelor’s degree Experience in academic advising, student services, program coordinator, and/or related higher education or workforce environment Knowledge of adult learner needs and/or nontraditional academic programming Strong project management skills Strong organizational, communication, and interpersonal skills Ability to manage multiple student populations and priorities simultaneously Preferred Master’s degree Experience working with non-traditional/adult learners and/or workforce development programs Familiarity with degree audit systems, student information systems, and advising technologies Experience building partnerships with external organizations (e.g., employers and community agencies) Core Competencies Operational coordination and attention to detail Relationship-building, student-centered advising approach Equity-minded practice and commitment to serving diverse student populations Ability to build relationships across internal and external stakeholders Ability to work independently and collaboratively across functions Work Environment & Expectations Primary work location is on campus Position requires occasional off-site work at partner locations, training sites, or program-related events Occasional evening or weekend hours to support adult learners and non-traditional student activities Physical Demands Ability to spend long durations of the workday sitting at a desk and working on a computer Must be able to lift up to 15 pounds at times Salary and FLSA Status Salary: $55,000-60,000 Schedule: Full-Time, 37.5 hours per week BENEFITS Comprehensive health, dental, and vision insurance Retirement plan with employer matching Professional development opportunities Tuition remission for employees and their dependents Employment at Dominican University is contingent upon the successful completion of background investigation. ABOUT DOMINICAN UNIVERSITY Dominican University is a Catholic, comprehensive teaching university located in River Forest, a suburb of Chicago, with an additional campus in Pilsen neighborhood of Chicago. Accredited by the Higher Learning Commission, Dominican University offers associate, baccalaureate, master’s, and doctoral degrees. It is highly regarded for its academic rigor, thoughtful accompaniment of students, intentional affordability, and values-based, relationship-centered learning environment. EXPECTATIONS FOR ALL DOMINICAN UNIVERSITY EMPLOYEES To support the University's mission of preparing students to pursue truth, give compassionate service, and participate in the creation of a more just and humane world, engage in university service work that promotes collaboration and community building. This includes participating in organizational initiatives, supporting campus events, contributing to community outreach, and fostering a diverse and inclusive environment through involvement in various staff-driven activities and groups. INSTITUTIONAL STATEMENT Dominican University is committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive academic community. We encourage applications from individuals of all backgrounds. Upon request, reasonable accommodation in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY STATEMENT Dominican University is an equal opportunity employer and encourages applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and protected veterans. Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by Dominican University | (708) 366-2490"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a78826752f2e2840152f6563b9f01df&id=8a7885aca18473fa01a1b2e97bfb6940": {
      "title": "Resident Assistant",
      "description": "Dominican University Careers Careers JOIN OUR TEAM Position: Resident Assistant Location: River Forest, IL Job Id: 2422 # of Openings: 1 TITLE: Resident Assistant DEPARTMENT: Office of Residence Life | Division of Student Affairs REPORTS TO: Residence Life Leadership Staff OPEN TO: Dominican University Undergraduate Students COMPENSATION: A monthly stipend paid from September through May Plus room and board package DATES AVAILABLE: 2027/28 Academic Year Position Description: The Resident Assistant (RA) is a student staff member who lives in one of the four residence halls on the Dominican University River Forest campus. Their primary purpose is to promote and enhance student’s social, educational, and personal development; assist in maintaining residence hall facilities and operations; and enforce university policies. Role Model and Student Leader RAs will serve as positive role models for all students. This includes but is not limited to modeling appropriate and mature behavior both on and off campus, as well as via digital and social media. RAs are expected to support the vision, mission, values, philosophies, and goals of the Department of Residence Life. RAs are responsible for abiding by, upholding and enforcing the Dominican Student Handbook and Code of Conduct including role modeling Caritas et Veritas , compassion and truth, as outlined in the Student Handbook and Code of Conduct Standards of Behavior: Integrity, Community, Social Justice, Respect and Responsibility. Community Development RAs should be visible, approachable, and regularly available to their residents and floor community. In the spirit of Caritas Veritas, the RA is responsible for supporting the unique needs of the individuals in their communities. In partnership with our residents, RAs will build communities focused on mutual respect and support that stimulate academic and personal growth for the community. RAs serve as a resource for residents and should be familiar with the services and opportunities provided by Residence Life, the Division of Student Affairs, and other university departments and offices. The RA will follow all programmatic and resident connection requirements as outlined in the Residence Life Programming Model . RAs will receive this curriculum, including the corresponding expectations, during fall training. RAs will mediate roommate agreements as needed and will facilitate conversations when roommate conflicts arise. RAs will keep supervisors informed of situations with roommates in their community. Collaboration and Team Member RAs are expected to maintain good communication with their fellow RAs and other residence life student leaders regarding community issues, activities and development. RAs should initiate and maintain awareness of and collaboration with campus partners, such as the Office of Student Involvement, University Ministry, Wellness Center, Center for Cultural Liberation, Campus Safety, faculty and administrative partners associated with the Learning Communities. RAs will honor the individual identities of their team by engaging in development opportunities to understand their own personal development. RAs will assume responsibility for other duties as communicated by the Department of Residence Life. Administrative Responsibilities RAs will participate in bi-annual training (2-3 weeks prior to the beginning of the fall semester, and one (1) week prior to the beginning of the spring semester – exact dates pending publication of academic calendar) and ongoing training throughout the year. The RA agrees to assist with annual departmental processes including but not limited to RA selection, health and safety inspections, fire drills, room selection, opening and closing of the halls. Which includes completing all necessary paperwork and follow-up in a timely manner. The RA must complete all necessary written reports properly and on time including but not limited to incident reports, program proposals/summaries, residential connections, duty logs, and all other forms assigned by Residence Life leadership. All written reports must be submitted based on Departmental and University expectations and be free from personal bias. RAs will complete performance evaluations each semester. Duty and Crisis Response RAs will serve on a rotating duty (on-call) schedule. RAs will respond to emergency and non-emergency situations, following appropriate protocols. RAs will assist in providing duty coverage during academic breaks and holidays when the University is closed. RAs are expected to adhere to duty protocols as described and provided during training and through supervision. RAs will enforce policies consistently and identify ways to remove bias from the response to student concerns and crises. Time Commitment The RA role is a one academic year commitment from August through May, (dates pending publication of 2027-2028 academic calendar). Must be able to work the full term of the position. The RA role requires an average of 20 hours of time per week including: Residence Hall Office/Community Hours Weekly or bi-weekly one-on-one meetings with their supervisor. On-call duty nights, weekdays (Sunday – Thursday), approximately 14 days per semester. 24-hour on-call, weekends (Friday – Saturday), Approximately 3 weekends per semester. Availability on Thursdays from 2:30 pm – 3:30 pm for mandatory bi-weekly Residence Life meetings. One academic break coverage period (Thanksgiving Break, Winter Break I, Winter Break II, Winter Break III, Winter IV, Spring Break, Easter Vacation (final coverage dates to be determined when academic calendar is published). At the conclusion of each semester, the RA will remain in the residence hall to support Residence Life staff for official closing of the building/area as determined by the Department of Residence Life (final dates to be determined when academic calendar is published). All RAs are required to be on campus and in their designated communities during blackout periods – August Training through the first weekend of September and January Training. Excluding official University break periods, a supervisor must approve and be informed of extended/significant time away from campus. In accepting the RA role, the RA agrees that the RA role assumes priority over other extra-curricular and co-curricular activities except for academics. The RA may be asked to limit activities should the activities impede upon or negatively affect the RA’s performance. Since activities and commitments may create time or priority conflicts, the RA will limit involvement in other leadership positions, employment, or outside activities to a maximum of twelve (12) hours per week. Any additional time commitments will need to be approved by their supervisor. RAs may be selected to return to the position pending successful rehire application and process. Requirements: Must be a student in good standing and not on Academic Notice or Disciplinary Probation Must have a minimum cumulative GPA of 2.75 to apply and must maintain a cumulative and term GPA of 2.75 to remain in good standing during the duration of employment; failure to maintain a cumulative and term GPA of 2.75 may impact continued employment. May hold no more than one executive board or leadership position outside of the Resident Assistant role. Commitments outside the Resident Assistant position must be discussed and reviewed with Residence Life supervisor. Must be full-time students during the entire duration of employment; failure to maintain a full-time course load may impact continued employment. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Working knowledge of Microsoft Office Products (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, etc.); Canva. Excellent communication, leadership, time management, and organizational skills. Ability to take initiative and work independently of direct supervision. Ability to work effectively with individuals from diverse communities and cultures. Employees shall adhere to all applicable rules and regulations of the University. Required Education Current Dominican University student. One (1) semester of living on campus is preferred. To apply for the position of Resident Assistant, please submit your resume, along with a cover letter responding to each of the questions below. Applications without these answers will not be considered. In the cover letter for the Resident Assistant position please answer the following questions. Please include your name and Dominican ID in the cover letter. Tell us about you. Which of your qualities would lead you to be successful in the Resident Assistant position? Resident Assistants are responsible for building connections within their communities. In what ways have you built meaningful connections thus far at Dominican? What does an inclusive community mean to you? If hired, how do you plan to foster a sense of inclusivity in your community? In what ways do you hope to develop your leadership skills as a Resident Assistant? Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by Dominican University | (708) 366-2490"
    },
    "https://recruitingbypaycor.com/career/JobIntroduction.action?clientId=8a78826752f2e2840152f6563b9f01df&id=8a7887aca18473d901a1c201a48d3a88": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Physical Education, Spring 2027",
      "description": "Dominican University Careers Careers JOIN OUR TEAM Position: Adjunct Faculty, Physical Education, Spring 2027 Location: River Forest, IL Job Id: 2424 # of Openings: 1 Adjunct Faculty, Physical Education, Spring 2027 Department: Rosary College of Arts, Education and Science Position Available: January 11, 2027 Position Summary: Dominican University is seeking a part-time Adjunct Faculty Member to teach the following: PHED 181 Introduction to Yoga (1 credit hour); course meets for one hour/week Learn postures to cultivate strength and flexibility, with exposure to related practices such as breath control and meditation. Course offered on a satisfactory/fail basis only. Required Qualifications: A bachelor's degree in the discipline or related field and at least one of the following: three years of tested experience relevant to course outcomes; OR additional professional credentials (i.e. licensure or certification in a relevant area) and a minimum of two years of tested experience relevant to course outcomes Preferred Qualifications: A Masters Degree in Physical Education or Kinesiology Responsibilities: Adjunct faculty are responsible for the following: Adhering to the university policies, procedures, and university calendar with respect to reporting information to university offices. Administering student evaluation and timely and accurately submit the required paperwork related to instruction, including grades and course evaluations. Grading weekly assignments, responding to student emails and phone calls promptly, and providing feedback in a timely manner. Preparing course materials and delivering assignments appropriate to the subject matter. Meeting with students and hosting office hours. Adhering to the goals and objectives of the course, as guided by the Department Chair. To Apply: Dominican University will accept applications for this position until it is filled. Please submit a (1) cover letter; and (2) CV/resume, including a list of references Official transcripts will be required of finalists; submission instructions will be provided to applicants as necessary. Offers will be pending a background screening. Physical Demands Ability to spend long durations of the workday sitting at a desk and working on a computer Must be able to lift up to 15 pounds at times Salary and FLSA Status Salary: $1000.00/semester FLSA Status: Exempt (salary); Part-time Faculty (non-tenure track) Employment at Dominican University is contingent upon the successful completion of background investigation. ABOUT DOMINICAN UNIVERSITY Dominican University is a Catholic, comprehensive teaching university located in River Forest, a suburb of Chicago, with an additional campus in Pilsen neighborhood of Chicago. Accredited by the Higher Learning Commission, Dominican University offers associate, baccalaureate, master’s, and doctoral degrees. It is highly regarded for its academic rigor, thoughtful accompaniment of students, intentional affordability, and values-based, relationship-centered learning environment. EXPECTATIONS FOR ALL DOMINICAN UNIVERSITY EMPLOYEES To support the University's mission of preparing students to pursue truth, give compassionate service, and participate in the creation of a more just and humane world, engage in university service work that promotes collaboration and community building. This includes participating in organizational initiatives, supporting campus events, contributing to community outreach, and fostering a diverse and inclusive environment through involvement in various staff-driven activities and groups. INSTITUTIONAL STATEMENT Dominican University is committed to fostering a diverse and inclusive academic community. We encourage applications from individuals of all backgrounds. Upon request, reasonable accommodation in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY STATEMENT Dominican University is an equal opportunity employer and encourages applications from women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and protected veterans. Apply for this Position Go back to the job list powered by Dominican University | (708) 366-2490"
    }
  },
